[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router4’s uplink interface to the core (eth5 on 172.25.1.0/24), not router3. Mechanism: PCs in Location C (192.168.200.0/24) use router4 (192.168.200.1) as default gateway. With router4’s upstream link down, router4 has no route toward Location A (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24), so it immediately returns ICMP net-unreachable to the PCs.

Localization and evidence:
- From pc11, ping to 172.16.10.4 returns “From 192.168.200.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” proving router4 is the rejecting gateway.
- On router4, eth5 (the link to router3) is DOWN while configured 172.25.1.1/24: “ip addr … eth5 state DOWN … inet 172.25.1.1/24.” Router4’s kernel route table only shows the directly connected 192.168.200.0/24, i.e., no upstream routes.
- Pings from router4 to router3 fail with “Network is unreachable,” consistent with eth5 down.
- Router3’s side is healthy: eth5 is UP with 172.25.1.2/24, it has RIP-learned routes toward 192.168.200.0/24 via 172.25.1.1, and pings to 192.168.200.1/11 fail with “Destination Host Unreachable” sourced from 172.25.1.2 (no L2 to router4), indicating only the far end is down.

Root cause: Router4’s interface eth5 (link_r4_r3 to router3) is down, isolating Location C from the rest of the network.

Corrective fix: Do not replace router3. Restore router4’s uplink: bring eth5 up and verify cabling on link_r4_r3 (or swap router4’s NIC/port if faulty). After eth5 is UP, RIP will reestablish and Location C will regain reachability to 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24. === END ===